#!/usr/bin/python3
"""Software: find, install and remove apps from Debian, FlickOS and Flathub.

The window shows apps, not packages: every program that publishes AppStream
data as a desktop app. The apt sources' apps come from the DEP-11 catalogs
that the `appstream` package's apt hook keeps in /var/lib/swcatalog (read
through libappstream and its system cache): Debian's, and FlickOS's
repository's two distributions, told apart by the catalog's origin
(APT_ORIGINS): FlickOS's own apps, and the community apps other developers
publish there, which FlickOS builds from their public source and reviews
(design ADR-004). Flathub's come from the appstream.xml that flatpak keeps
for the remote (read with the standard library). An app that is in more
than one shows once, and an apt source is the default: it shares libraries
with the system, takes less disk and memory, and the automatic updates
already cover it. Flathub is offered next to it, for the newer version.
Design and measurements: docs/design/flickos-software.md.

The front page's shelves (Made for FlickOS, Apps to start with) are
featured.json: the package's copy (FEATURED_FILE), replaced by the newest
one from FEATURED_URL, fetched at most every FEATURED_HOURS and kept in
the cache folder. That request carries nothing about its user (ADR-009).

The window never reads the catalogs itself (libappstream needs seconds and
hundreds of MB for that): `index` turns them into two files in
~/.cache/flickos/software/, index.json (one row per app, with every alias,
for the lists and the search) and pages-N.jsonl (descriptions, screenshots,
versions, read one line at a time). The window runs `index` in a child when
a catalog is newer than the index and shows the old one meanwhile. When
Debian's catalog is missing (the live image has no apt lists) or Flathub's
is missing or wasn't checked for REFRESH_DAYS, it runs `refresh`, which
asks PackageKit for apt's lists (that also runs the appstream hook) and
libflatpak for Flathub's catalog; polkit lets an active session do both
without a password.

Installing and removing run as child commands (`install`, `remove`) that
print one JSON line per step, so the window never loads PackageKit or
libflatpak itself and a second window can't race the first. Apt sources'
apps go through PackageKit, which asks for the password (installing keeps it for a
while, removing asks every time; Q8 in the design keeps it that way). Flathub
apps go through libflatpak into the system installation, which needs no
password for an administrator (flatpak's own polkit rule). Before either,
`plan` simulates the change without a password: what comes with an app
(Flathub's runtimes, with their sizes) or what goes with it. Nothing that
FlickOS's desktop needs is ever removed (PROTECTED_PREFIXES and
flickos-desktop's Depends): `remove` and `install` refuse such a change
themselves, not only the window.

Styles for the desktop are apps here too (docs/design/flickos-ricing.md,
ADR-006, ADR-013): an apt package whose metainfo is an add-on extending
flickos-layout.desktop (STYLE_EXTENDS) and names its style folder
(STYLE_KEY) shows under Styles only; installed, its page offers Try (until
logout) and Use instead of Open, through flickos-layout. FlickOS's own
style packages (STYLE_PREFIX) can be removed, unlike FlickOS's others.
Flatpak apps update with the automatic updates (ADR-008):
flickos-software-update.timer runs /usr/libexec/flickos/software-update once
a day, which follows apt's own settings (Update-Package-Lists for Flathub's
catalog, Unattended-Upgrade for the apps), so flickos-control's Updates page
turns both on and off. It does nothing in the live session.

This program owns no settings. It sends nothing about its user (ADR-009):
icons and screenshots are fetched from the addresses in the catalogs and
kept in ~/.cache/flickos/software/images/ for IMAGE_DAYS.

Commands:
  (none), show [--search WORDS | --app ID | --installed]
                    open the window (one per session)
  search WORDS...   print matching apps: id, sources, installed, name, summary
  list [--installed | --category ID | --shelves]
                    print apps like search, or the categories: id, name, apps,
                    or the front page's shelves: shelf, app id, name
  info ID           print an app's page: field, value
  index [--force]   rebuild the index when a catalog changed (or always)
  refresh [--force] fetch catalogs and featured.json when missing or old (or
                    all), then index
  plan [--json] [--source SOURCE] [--remove] ID
                    what installing (removing) the app would bring (take)
  install [--json] [--source SOURCE] ID
  remove [--json] [--source SOURCE] ID
                    install or remove an app; --json prints one JSON object
                    per line ({"event": "progress"|"done"|"error"|"cancelled"})
                    and SIGTERM cancels. SOURCE: debian, flickos, community
                    or flathub (SOURCES)

Exit codes: 0 ok, 1 error (or cancelled), 2 usage error.
"""

import argparse
